nough time divorced from fear, these Hungers mark a transition from simple desire for security into desire for itself, a thing that is collected because it fills you, it satisfies you, maybe even makes you happy, a thing that spirals into itself and shells outward into homes, communities, cities, nations-civilization itself. Go through any place in the world you would like, you can see how the stones of each are laid in pursuit of those Hungers-wise civilizations even understand that transgression is a needful thing, as law and order can be cloying and rotting things, and that a little freedom and challenge in the populace releases pressures that might otherwise stress, strain, and break a society (look at civilization's long and sometimes complicated relationship with alcohol).<br /> <br /> As a rule, as a general thing, it is understood as a good and meet thing to sate Hunger-we all need a measure of these things to be whole and secure in our own right, and to be wielded to foster and build it up for others. But it's one thing to sate, quite another to glut. If/When we do things like the family write ups for Hunger-if the key to Families is <i>and that's all right</i>, for Hunger it's <i>and that is all well and good.....up to a point</i>. Some will accrue their Hunger to an excessive amount, til they are divorced from genuine fear and their humanity, some will starve others of their Hungers. Beasts will sometimes pick targets because their abundance of Satiety is leading them to mute their psychospiritual senses and connections, sometimes they'll go after someone because they're being an asshole in pursuit or success with their Hunger.....or sometimes because they just want it.<br /> <br /> The Three Main Sections I'd have in a new core would be something like To Take And Waste (since at a minimum every Beast must take the representation of someone's Hunger to sate their own, and the closest kin to that is to destroy it), To Keep and Build (since a lot of Beasts will of course incorproate their gains into their Lives and Lairs, and build from there), and To Return and Gift (because sometimes, the point is that someone just needs to remember what it was like without the blanket, and thus a Beast can give it back after the fact, or otherwise gift it to worthier, wiser people-or just whoever they want to).<br /> <br /> The Insatiable have the clearest relationship to Hunger, namely in that in their over-satiation, they demonstrate the folly of trying to divorce away from fear, and with it the world and the animal self-that no amount of Satiation can <i>actually</i> make you safe, secure, comfortable. Heroes contrast that, and almost prove the Insatiable's point-in that, since they have such an abundance of Hunger and it's Satiation, how could any think they're afraid, putting the cart before the horse and presenting persona as self.<br /> <br /> But yeah, the tl;dr of it all is &amp;quot;If Family is Fear, Hunger is Security&amp;quot;. Hunger is the reason Beasts have just as much in common with the Spring Court as they do the Autumn Court.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh nice,very nice. I think that description makes it similar enough to a vampire desire for blood,or a changeling desire for Glamour for people new ish to the CofD to.understand it","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4621-nicolas-milioni","name":"Nicolas Milioni","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4621_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3605"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}